00:36:01,030 --> 00:36:06,800
They landed on, if you do it, keep it super short, laser focused, and make sure it adds real value, not just fluff.

734
00:36:07,050 --> 00:36:10,320
It's got to enhance the point you're making, not derail the presentation.

735
00:36:10,420 --> 00:36:14,070
Then they played around with contrast and color backgrounds to emphasize different sections.

736
00:36:14,130 --> 00:36:15,910
But again, they were cautious.

737
00:36:16,280 --> 00:36:18,680
Keep it tasteful, not an assault on the eyes.

738
00:36:18,880 --> 00:36:19,740
Subtlety is key.

739
00:36:20,060 --> 00:36:20,700
Big picture.

740
00:36:21,080 --> 00:36:23,910
Each slide should flow logically from the one before.

741
00:36:23,970 --> 00:36:25,570
You know, like a good story.

742
00:36:25,590 --> 00:36:26,510
You're building a narrative.

743
00:36:26,730 --> 00:36:30,120
It guides your audience along, makes it easy to follow your thought process.

744
00:36:30,240 --> 00:36:32,040
And of course, practice, practice, practice.

745
00:36:32,080 --> 00:36:35,450
With your actual slides, get the timing down, smooth out those transitions.

746
00:36:35,510 --> 00:36:36,690
It makes a huge difference.

747
00:36:36,850 --> 00:36:37,330
Absolutely.

748
00:36:37,370 --> 00:36:39,990
You got to see how all the pieces fit together in real time.

749
00:36:40,210 --> 00:36:45,280
OK, now let's talk about you, the presenter, mastering your voice and body language.

750
00:36:45,320 --> 00:36:51,650
They were big on projection, you know, projecting your voice so you command attention, but not in a shouty, aggressive way.

751
00:36:52,210 --> 00:36:56,680
It's about being clear, being heard, but also being approachable.

752
00:36:56,860 --> 00:36:59,590
It's that balance between authority and warmth.

753
00:36:59,630 --> 00:37:06,140
And pacing, like controlling your speed, not too fast that people can't keep up, not so slow that they fall asleep.

754
00:37:06,300 --> 00:37:07,800
It's got to be just right.

755
00:37:08,020 --> 00:37:11,430
It's about respecting your audience's ability to process what you're saying.

756
00:37:11,650 --> 00:37:14,650
This one, we all struggle with this, those filler words.

757
00:37:15,970 --> 00:37:20,320
You know, actually, they suggested replacing them with pauses, mindful pauses.

758
00:37:20,620 --> 00:37:23,030
Yeah, it makes you sound more thoughtful, more in control.

759
00:37:23,140 --> 00:37:31,530
And I think it gives people a moment to digest too, you know, varying your tone is another key one, you know, to emphasize certain points, keep it interesting.

760
00:37:31,970 --> 00:37:34,960
If you're monotone the whole time, people tune out.

761
00:37:34,980 --> 00:37:37,420
It's like listening to a song with no melody, just one note.

762
00:37:37,820 --> 00:37:38,430
Exactly.

763
00:37:38,470 --> 00:37:43,950
And facial expressions, smile, show enthusiasm, you know, be human.

764
00:37:43,990 --> 00:37:46,440
It makes you relatable, makes people want to listen.

765
00:37:46,520 --> 00:37:49,380
Let your face reflect your passion for the topic.

766
00:37:49,450 --> 00:38:18,000
hand gestures they're good right to emphasize points but not too much you don't want to be a windmill yeah you want to use them deliberately not just be flailing around and this was smart they actually recommended recording yourself practicing so you can see your posture your eye contact all that it can be eye-opening for sure oh it can be brutal but so helpful it shows you what you're really projecting speaking with passion this came up a lot if you don't believe what you're saying why should they authenticity is contagious

767
00:38:18,090 --> 00:38:19,910
It's that energy that draws people in.

768
00:38:20,010 --> 00:38:27,860
And then, of course, nerves, deep breathing exercises, classic, but they work just to calm yourself down before you start.

769
00:38:28,180 --> 00:38:29,140
Yeah, center yourself.

770
00:38:29,620 --> 00:38:35,560
They also suggested practicing those transitions between sections so they feel smooth, not clunky.

771
00:38:35,600 --> 00:38:38,890
It helps your audience follow along, understand the flow of your ideas.

772
00:38:39,150 --> 00:38:41,550
And after a complex point, just pause.

773
00:38:42,150 --> 00:38:44,130
Give people a moment to process, you know?

774
00:38:44,330 --> 00:38:45,550
Respect the information.

775
00:38:46,250 --> 00:38:47,220
Let it sink in.

776
00:38:47,200 --> 00:38:50,460
A little humor can be great, but they cautioned.

777
00:38:50,580 --> 00:38:51,700
Make sure it fits the tone.

778
00:38:51,780 --> 00:38:52,980
Make sure it's not offensive.

779
00:38:53,180 --> 00:38:54,690
Yeah, you don't want to alienate anyone.

780
00:38:54,710 --> 00:38:55,870
Warm up your voice, too.

781
00:38:55,930 --> 00:39:01,760
Do some articulation exercises so you're nice and clear, especially if you're using any technical terms.

782
00:39:02,280 --> 00:39:04,460
And then practice, practice, practice.

783
00:39:04,480 --> 00:39:05,740
Until it's second nature.

784
00:39:05,720 --> 00:39:07,010
Okay, Q&A time.

785
00:39:07,490 --> 00:39:09,090
This is where it can get real, right?

786
00:39:10,640 --> 00:39:21,420
They were all about prepping for those tough questions, like actually thinking about what people might ask and having some answers ready saves you from that deer in the headlights moment.

787
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:22,660
Preparation is key.

788
00:39:23,070 --> 00:39:24,350
It builds your confidence.

789
00:39:24,370 --> 00:39:27,030
And what if you get a question that's like confrontational?

790
00:39:27,320 --> 00:39:32,290
They said take a breath, stay calm, address the issue head on, but don't get defensive.

791
00:39:32,410 --> 00:39:33,330
It shows you're in control.

792
00:39:33,530 --> 00:39:34,670
Have some evidence ready too.

793
00:39:34,890 --> 00:39:38,480
Data, backup slides, you know, if you need to go deeper.

794
00:39:38,600 --> 00:39:39,680
Shows you've done your homework.

795
00:39:39,820 --> 00:39:40,640
And this was a good one.

796
00:39:41,060 --> 00:39:43,350
If there's a valid criticism, acknowledge it.

797
00:39:43,550 --> 00:39:44,450
Be transparent.

798
00:39:44,550 --> 00:39:45,970
It actually builds trust.

799
00:39:46,090 --> 00:39:46,950
It shows you're human.

800
00:39:46,970 --> 00:39:49,060
You're not afraid to admit when someone has a point.

801
00:39:49,260 --> 00:39:53,230
And if a question is, like, super vague, rephrase it.

802
00:39:53,710 --> 00:39:55,610
Clarify what they're really asking.

803
00:39:55,630 --> 00:39:56,980
Make sure you're on the same page.

804
00:39:56,960 --> 00:39:59,720
Body language matters, even during Q&A.

805
00:40:00,740 --> 00:40:05,050
Keep eye contact, stand tall, you know, project confidence.

806
00:40:05,130 --> 00:40:06,200
It's all part of the message.

807
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:08,460
What if a question goes totally off topic?

808
00:40:08,820 --> 00:40:12,290
Gently steer it back to the main point, but be polite, you know?

809
00:40:12,330 --> 00:40:12,670
Right.

810
00:40:12,970 --> 00:40:14,800
Keep it relevant without being dismissive.

811
00:40:14,960 --> 00:40:18,730
If someone has a totally different viewpoint, they said try to find common ground.

812
00:40:19,390 --> 00:40:20,930
Start from a place of agreement.

813
00:40:21,270 --> 00:40:23,630
It can de-escalate things, make it more of a conversation.

814
00:40:23,850 --> 00:40:27,460
Always thank the person who asked the question, even if it was like a real zinger.

815
00:40:28,140 --> 00:40:29,060
It shows respect.

816
00:40:29,080 --> 00:40:29,620
And guess what?

817
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:31,200
Practice Q&A too.

818
00:40:31,860 --> 00:40:33,650
With colleagues, friends, whoever.

819
00:40:33,990 --> 00:40:35,190
Get used to thinking on your feet.

820
00:40:35,530 --> 00:40:38,350
It's a skill, just like any other part of presenting.

821
00:40:38,380 --> 00:40:44,190
They even talked about bridging techniques, you know, to subtly guide those questions back to your main point.

822
00:40:44,260 --> 00:40:47,480
It keeps you in control, but in a way that feels natural.

823
00:40:47,700 --> 00:40:51,970
Have some notes handy, too, in case you need to jog your memory on a key fact or figure.

824
00:40:51,990 --> 00:40:52,750
It's like a safety net.

825
00:40:53,510 --> 00:40:54,490
And be patient.

826
00:40:54,630 --> 00:40:57,660
Listen to each question carefully so people feel heard.

827
00:40:57,700 --> 00:40:58,940
Don't rush them.

828
00:40:59,090 --> 00:40:59,890
It shows respect.

829
00:41:00,070 --> 00:41:02,170
So much goes into a good Q&A, doesn't it?

830
00:41:02,190 --> 00:41:03,490
It's a whole other art form.

831
00:41:03,520 --> 00:41:07,700
Okay, let's talk structure, how to organize your content so it makes sense, it flows.

832
00:41:08,160 --> 00:41:12,550
They emphasized logical order, like a clear path through your arguments.

833
00:41:12,650 --> 00:41:14,750
It's a roadmap, right, so people don't get lost.

834
00:41:14,930 --> 00:41:15,590
Exactly.

835
00:41:16,070 --> 00:41:21,800
And grouping related points together under themes, that was another one, helps people see the connections.

836
00:41:22,020 --> 00:41:23,220
It makes it easier to remember.

837
00:41:23,420 --> 00:41:28,830
They liked starting with a high-level overview, you know, before diving deep, like setting the stage.

838
00:41:28,890 --> 00:41:32,930
It's like giving people the big picture, the lay the land before you zoom in.

839
00:41:33,280 --> 00:41:36,120
Then present your most important arguments first.

840
00:41:36,620 --> 00:41:38,460
You know, the really strong stuff.

841
00:41:39,220 --> 00:41:40,060
Capture attention.

842
00:41:40,460 --> 00:41:42,450
Front load it while people are still fresh.

843
00:41:42,780 --> 00:41:44,950
And don't go too crazy with those sub points.

844
00:41:45,090 --> 00:41:46,490
You don't want to overwhelm people.

845
00:41:46,530 --> 00:41:48,410
Pick the most relevant ones.

846
00:41:48,630 --> 00:41:50,590
Yeah, less is more sometimes.

847
00:41:50,870 --> 00:41:55,320
To make sure everyone's with you, they considered adding a quick recap after each section.

848
00:41:55,740 --> 00:41:56,800
Just a little reminder.

849
00:41:57,040 --> 00:41:58,880
It reinforces those key takeaways.

850
00:41:58,910 --> 00:42:01,900
Those transitions between sections, they were really big on that.

851
00:42:02,200 --> 00:42:05,040
Make them smooth, use clear transition statements.

852
00:42:05,260 --> 00:42:07,100
It's about guiding people along the journey.

853
00:42:07,260 --> 00:42:08,050
And this was cool.

854
00:42:08,630 --> 00:42:11,450
After you make a major point, use a real-life example.

855
00:42:11,770 --> 00:42:14,820
A story, a case study, something concrete.

856
00:42:14,970 --> 00:42:15,880
Makes it more relatable.

857
00:42:16,200 --> 00:42:18,520
Each slide, one key takeaway.

858
00:42:18,580 --> 00:42:19,400
That was another rule.

859
00:42:19,540 --> 00:42:20,480
Keep it focused.

860
00:42:20,500 --> 00:42:25,810
And then that ending, building towards a strong conclusion that brings everything together.

861
00:42:26,090 --> 00:42:27,750
It's like landing the plane smoothly.

862
00:42:27,950 --> 00:42:35,060
To help people along, they suggested verbal cues, like actually saying, key point one is this, or moving on to key point two.

863
00:42:35,160 --> 00:42:37,330
Those little signposts can make a difference.

