Last step before you actually present.

868
00:42:47,640 --> 00:42:49,250
Go back and look for redundancy.

869
00:42:49,870 --> 00:42:52,530
Cut out anything that's not absolutely essential.

870
00:42:52,790 --> 00:42:53,330
Make it tight.

871
00:42:53,470 --> 00:42:54,530
Make every point count.

872
00:42:54,680 --> 00:42:59,530
That whole structure thing, it's about taking people on a clear, logical journey, isn't it?

873
00:42:59,550 --> 00:43:00,010
Exactly.

874
00:43:00,150 --> 00:43:01,010
It's about flow.

875
00:43:01,430 --> 00:43:02,690
All right, let's talk visuals.

876
00:43:02,830 --> 00:43:06,800
Not just any visuals, though, using them strategically to make your presentation better.

877
00:43:07,280 --> 00:43:10,860
They were all about charts and diagrams, especially for stats.

878
00:43:11,740 --> 00:43:13,970
Makes that data less intimidating.

879
00:43:14,120 --> 00:43:15,010
Yeah, brings it to life.

880
00:43:15,370 --> 00:43:20,570
High contrast colors for charts, they said, so everyone can see them, even in the back row.

881
00:43:20,730 --> 00:43:21,670
Accessibility matters.

882
00:43:21,930 --> 00:43:23,010
But don't go overboard.

883
00:43:23,070 --> 00:43:26,040
Don't cram in a million visuals just for the sake of it.

884
00:43:26,440 --> 00:43:28,240
Make sure each one has a purpose.

885
00:43:28,680 --> 00:43:31,320
It should contribute to your message, not be a distraction.

886
